# pip — real-world examples

## Install packages

```sh
pip install SomePackage

# Install a specific version / version range
pip install -I SomePackage1==1.1.0 'SomePackage2>=1.0.4'
```

## Requirements files

```sh
# Freeze current environment and reinstall elsewhere
pip freeze > requirements.txt
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## Inspect packages

```sh
# Show details of a package (version, location, dependencies)
pip show SomePackage

# List outdated packages
pip list --outdated
```

## Upgrade all outdated packages

```sh
# via http://stackoverflow.com/a/3452888
pip freeze --local | grep -v '^\-e' | cut -d = -f 1 | xargs -n1 pip install -U
```

## Search for packages

```sh
# NOTE: `pip search` was disabled on PyPI; browse https://pypi.org instead
pip search SomePackage
```

## Homelab (OpenSUSE Tumbleweed) notes

```sh
# Tumbleweed marks system Python as externally managed — use a venv
python3 -m venv ~/venvs/tools
source ~/venvs/tools/bin/activate
pip install ansible-lint

# Per-user install without a venv
pip install --user yt-dlp

# Prefer zypper for system-wide Python packages
sudo zypper install python3-requests
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-r <file>` | Install from requirements file |
| `-U` | Upgrade to newest version |
| `-I` | Ignore installed packages (force reinstall) |
| `--user` | Install into user site-packages |
| `-e <path>` | Editable install (develop mode) |
